            <div xml:id="d144">

                <pb facs="0208" n="200" xml:id="pg200"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.452/44</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The High Commissioner of the Government of India at
                        London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Noon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the Division
                        of Near Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Murray</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-07-01">July 1, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Wallace Murray</hi>: It was a great pleasure
                    to have met you this morning and to have discussed the trade treaty with your
                    officers, Messrs. Ayling [<hi rendition="#italic">Alling</hi>], Parker and
                    Turkel. During these discussions, it was informally agreed that the point of
                    view of the Government of India regarding a special treatment to be accorded to
                    limitrophe countries was in accordance with the usual practice and that the
                    State Department will have no objection to this principle being incorporated in
                    this treaty.</p>
                <p>The second point we discussed was the exploitation of mineral resources. In this
                    respect, your officers were keen that the U. S. A. citizens should have an equal
                    treatment with the United Kingdom subjects. I am passing this information on to
                    my own Government and I hope that before long the two countries will be able to
                    come to a final decision.</p>
                <p>Assuring you [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Firoz Noon</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
